Native Amiga LoadSeg placement regression
=======================================

Build with test-dos-hunkplacement or test-dos-hunkplacement-quick. The native
Amiga DOS component test group selects this test; it requires a stock 2 MiB
Chip RAM configuration without Fast RAM, with most memory free.

The test writes three small Hunk files to RAM:. Each reserves a 192 KiB hunk
and records its stack pointer before returning 42. Their memory requests are
ordinary, Chip RAM, and an extended Chip RAM request explicitly specifying
MEMF_REVERSE.

Reserve the largest Chip RAM block, leaving 128 KiB for filesystem activity,
then free two 256 KiB regions separated by a retained allocation. Reserve any
other blocks large enough to hold the fixture. LoadSeg must place ordinary
hunks in the lower suitable region and honor the explicit reverse request in
the upper region. RunCommand must execute each fixture and return 42, using
the lower suitable free region for its ordinary command stack allocation.

The former unconditional MEMF_REVERSE in LoadSeg places all three hunks in
the upper region, failing the two ordinary placement checks. Forced reverse
command stacks fail three additional checks. The corrected routines pass all
18 checks. No production allocation vectors are patched.

The fixture paths RAM:hunkplacement-any, RAM:hunkplacement-chip, and
RAM:hunkplacement-reverse are temporary and removed on exit. Run one instance
at a time on a quiet test machine. Do not use these names for unrelated files.
